# Map borders: annotation_borders

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`annotation_borders()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/annotation_borders.html), which
covers `borders()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`annotation_borders()` is a quick way to put map data from the `maps` package onto a plot: a
layer of polygons outlining the world's countries, by default, or another of the package's maps,
such as the US states, to give rough reference lines to data placed by longitude and latitude.
Its `xlim` and `ylim` pick the part of the map to take. `borders()` is its old name, now
deprecated. Giggly has none of it yet.

## State capitals

```r
if (require("maps")) {
data(us.cities)
capitals <- subset(us.cities, capital == 2)
ggplot(capitals, aes(long, lat)) +
  annotation_borders("state") +
  geom_point(aes(size = pop)) +
  scale_size_area() +
  coord_quickmap()
}
```

`require()` runs each example only when the `maps` package is installed; `capital == 2` picks
the state capitals from its table of US cities.

Not in Giggly yet: map data from the `maps` package, `annotation_borders()` and `coord_quickmap()`.

## The world around them

```r
if (require("maps")) {
# Same map, with some world context
ggplot(capitals, aes(long, lat)) +
  annotation_borders("world", xlim = c(-130, -60), ylim = c(20, 50)) +
  geom_point(aes(size = pop)) +
  scale_size_area() +
  coord_quickmap()
}
```

Not in Giggly yet: map data from the `maps` package, `annotation_borders()` and its `xlim` and
`ylim` options, and `coord_quickmap()`.
